    <string name="news_tips_title">Expert tips</string>
    <string name="news_tip_clean">How to keep your aligners clean</string>
    <string name="news_tip_clean_body">Rinse aligners every time you remove them, brush them gently with a soft toothbrush (no toothpaste — it scratches), and soak daily in clear aligner cleaner or a mild antibacterial solution. Never use hot water: it warps the plastic and changes the fit.</string>
    <string name="news_tip_remove">Removing trays comfortably</string>
    <string name="news_tip_remove_body">Always remove aligners from the back molars first, using a small hook motion with your fingernails or an orthodontic removal tool. Pull upward (upper) or downward (lower) at the very back and work forward. This puts less stress on the front teeth, which are the most sensitive.</string>
    <string name="news_tip_switch">When NOT to switch trays</string>
    <string name="news_tip_switch_body">Don\'t advance to the next tray early if the current one still feels loose in some spots or doesn\'t fully seat — attachment tracking matters more than the calendar. And if you switch a couple of days late, don\'t rush; keep each subsequent tray for its full period and let the plan catch up.</string>
